            <ArticleTitle>[Association of active commuting with obesity: findings from the Chilean National Health Survey 2009-2010].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>837-844</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0034-98872017000700837</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4067/s0034-98872017000700837</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Active commuting is associated with a lower risk for obesity in developed countries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the association between active commuting and obesity risk in Chile.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Active commuting was measured using the Global Physical Activity Questionnaire (GPAQ v2) in 5,293 participants from the Chilean National Health Survey 2009-2010. Body mass index (BMI) and waist circumference (WC) were the study outcomes. The association between active commuting and obesity was investigated using linear and logistic regression analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Thirty four percent of responders [95% confidence intervals (CI): 32.6-35.1] were passive commuters. Active commuters had a lower BMI and WC than their passive counterparts. Thirty minutes increment in active commuting were associated with a -0.20 kg.m-2 lower BMI [95% CI: -0.33 to -0.07, p &lt; 0.01] and a -076 cm lower WC [95% CI: -1.08 to -0.43, p &lt; 0.01]. The odds of having a BMI &gt; 25 kg.m-2 was 0.93 [95% CI: 0.88 to 0.98, p = 0.01] per every 30 minutes' increment in active commuting, whereas the odds for central obesity was 0.87 [95% CI: 0.82 to 0.92, p &lt; 0.01].</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Active commuting is associated with a lower adiposity and lower risk for obesity in Chilean adults.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
